        "chapter": "Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",
        "solution": "<p>A plasmid is an extrachromosomal, usually circular, double-stranded DNA of bacteria (also of yeast) that replicates independently of the chromosomal DNA.</p> <p>A foreign DNA piece joined to it is carried into the host cell and multiplied with it, so plasmids (e.g. pBR322) are used as cloning vectors.</p> <p>Genes are joined by DNA ligase, not by a plasmid; a plasmid is DNA, not mRNA; \"autotrophic fragment\" has no meaning here.</p>"
