        "solution": "<p>Restriction endonucleases were found as defence enzymes of bacteria.</p> <p>When a bacteriophage infects a bacterium, these enzymes cut the foreign viral DNA, while the bacterium's own DNA is protected by methylation.</p> <p>By doing so they limit (restrict) the multiplication of the phage inside the host, and that is the origin of the name; cutting at specific sites is how they work, not why they are called restriction enzymes.</p> <p>So they are named so because they destroy viral DNA and restrict virus growth in bacteria.</p>"
