        "subject": "Biology",
        "chapter": "Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",
        "solution": "<p>Restriction endonucleases occur naturally in bacteria.</p> <p>They cut the DNA of invading bacteriophages at specific recognition sites, while the bacterium protects its own DNA by methylating those sites; this restriction–modification system is a defence mechanism.</p> <p>Synthesis of DNA is done by DNA polymerase and sealing of fragments by DNA ligase, and these enzymes are not a eukaryotic defence system.</p> <p>So restriction enzymes are made by bacteria as part of their defence.</p>"
